  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Vudatta Suresh vs State Of Ap on 30 December, 2019 Hon'Ble Sri Justice Rakesh Kumar Criminal Petition No.7986 Of 2019 Order: 1. Heard Sri Raja Reddy Koneti, learned counsel for the petitioners, and learned Public Prosecutor. 2. The petitioners have invoked the inherent jurisdiction of this Court under Section 482 of the Cod",
    "facts": "Criminal Petition No.7986 Of 2019 Order: 1. Heard Sri Raja Reddy Koneti, learned counsel for the petitioners, and learned Public Prosecutor. 2. The petitioners have invoked the inherent jurisdiction of this Court under Section 482 of the Cod 3. Reliance has been placed on an unreported judgment, dated 18.12.2019, passed in Criminal Petition No.5421 of 2019 and other connected cases and also a common order passed by a learned single Judge of this Court, dated 27.08.2018, in Criminal Petition No.3731 of 2018 and other connected cases.",
    "arguments": "4. By way of placing the charge sheet, learned counsel for the petitioners has argued that save except recovery of tobacco products, which are prohibited under the FSS Act, no other cognizable offence is made out. In present matter, it appears that the Police with a view to usurp jurisdiction of lodging FIR, in a case of recovery of tobacco, which is prohibited under the FSS Act, has mechanically incorporated Sections 273 and 188 R/w.34 of IPC. He submits that since in an identical situation, this Court has RK,J interfered with the FIR and also during initial investigation, this Court may pass similar order. 5. Learned Public Prosecutor has tried to persuade the Court that if a cognizable offence under the provisions of IPC is made out along with the offences under the FSS Act, the Police is having jurisdiction to lodge the FIR and investigate the same.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "4. By way of placing the charge sheet, learned counsel for the petitioners has argued that save except recovery of tobacco products, which are prohibited under the FSS Act, no other cognizable offence is made out. In present matter, it appears that the Police with a view to usurp jurisdiction of lodging FIR, in a case of recovery of tobacco, which is prohibited under the FSS Act, has mechanically incorporated Sections 273 and 188 R/w.34 of IPC. He submits that since in an identical situation, this Court has RK,J interfered with the FIR and also during initial investigation, this Court may pass similar order. 5.",
    "respondent_arguments": "Learned Public Prosecutor has tried to persuade the Court that if a cognizable offence under the provisions of IPC is made out along with the offences under the FSS Act, the Police is having jurisdiction to lodge the FIR and investigate the same.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
